  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14144297/assets/6468ba15e8f280b41dbf6559f257ce09b87c0327a3b5e3198e4472b3abb43583.png)\n\nUse the model above to determine the missing number in the following division problem. \n\n$$\n3÷\\boxed{\\,\\,}=9\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "Let's start by interpreting our model:\n\n- The model shows $3$ wholes. Each whole is split into *sixths*.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14144297/assets/048796929d43fa5d1494c5759ef5a679c40d7a21345b05c92029d6c35f0e826f.png)\n\n \n\n- The sixths are grouped, and each group contains $\\frac{2}{6}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14144297/assets/e7e1d96565cab466f16deddba4327aacc7d184e322f22a341490839cb3e57d08.png)\n\n \n\n- There are $9$ groups in total.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14144297/assets/ecd00d4db8e6cbda3a383367f2e5fa95dbd61f264dab6fd001342d40221d7b48.png)\n\n \n\nSo, if we divide $3$ wholes into groups of $\\frac{2}{6},$ we get $9$ groups in total.\n\nTherefore, the model represents the following division problem:\n\n$$\n3÷\\boxed{\\frac{2}{6}}=9\n$$\n\nSo, the missing number is $\\frac{2}{6}.$",
